-Two released DCSM checkpoints, locally called ObjA and COCO, score context crops using ordinary CLIP features and an already trained CNN. The adapter supplies a 197-by-30 token--patch similarity matrix including CLS, with two convolutional layers and a 128-unit hidden fully connected layer. It inserts checkpoint functional embeddings using inherited whitespace positions and standardizes similarities jointly across candidate crops for a query. Tokenizer alignment is not independently certified; normalization makes scores candidate-batch dependent. No new training is performed here, but these scorers are not training-free components. **Modified readouts with the learned CNN were not evaluated.**
+Two released DCSM checkpoints [@kang2025dcsm], locally called ObjA and COCO, score context crops using ordinary CLIP features and an already trained CNN. The adapter supplies an image-position-by-text-position matrix (197-by-30), including image CLS and text BOS/EOS/padding positions, with two convolutional layers and a 128-unit hidden fully connected layer. It inserts checkpoint functional embeddings using inherited whitespace positions and standardizes over all entries of the candidate-by-channel-by-image-position-by-text-position tensor for a query. Tokenizer alignment is not independently certified; normalization makes scores candidate-batch dependent. No new training is performed here, but these scorers are not training-free components. Modified readouts with the learned CNN were not evaluated. Local filenames and implementation provenance are recorded in the non-public supplement; checkpoint hashes and release versions remain unavailable.
 
 OWL candidate scoring matches predicted boxes to each supplied candidate at IoU at least 0.5 and takes the maximum matching query logit. If no prediction matches, the best-IoU prediction supplies its logit minus 10. OWL selection thus includes localization and matching effects, unlike directly scoring supplied crops.
 
